1
00:00:00,091 --> 00:00:03,099
He was born Daood Gilani, in Washington,

2
00:00:03,385 --> 00:00:05,625
D.C., in 1960.

3
00:00:06,042 --> 00:00:08,672
His father was a Pakistani broadcaster.

4
00:00:09,089 --> 00:00:11,523
His mother was an American from a wealthy

5
00:00:11,536 --> 00:00:12,786
Philadelphia family.

6
00:00:14,027 --> 00:00:15,732
Then came Pakistan.

7
00:00:16,137 --> 00:00:17,531
He was still an infant.

8
00:00:18,703 --> 00:00:20,773
As a boy he went to Cadet College

9
00:00:20,773 --> 00:00:21,854
Hasan Abdal,

10
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:24,576
a military prep school in Punjab.

11
00:00:24,992 --> 00:00:26,724
There he met a classmate named

12
00:00:26,724 --> 00:00:29,471
Tahawwur Rana — the front man of our last

13
00:00:29,484 --> 00:00:29,914
file.

14
00:00:30,331 --> 00:00:33,026
At seventeen, Gilani moved back to America

15
00:00:33,039 --> 00:00:34,380
to live with his mother.

16
00:00:34,771 --> 00:00:36,594
He spoke Urdu and English.

17
00:00:36,997 --> 00:00:38,625
He could be charming in both.

18
00:00:39,790 --> 00:00:40,663
Two countries.

19
00:00:41,079 --> 00:00:42,121
Two languages.

20
00:00:43,347 --> 00:00:44,844
Eventually, two names.

21
00:00:45,262 --> 00:00:47,137
He would later take his mother's maiden

22
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:49,494
name, and become David Headley.

23
00:00:49,911 --> 00:00:52,762
One US counter-terrorism official, quoted

24
00:00:52,775 --> 00:00:54,650
by ProPublica and NBC,

25
00:00:55,249 --> 00:00:56,773
summed him up in a single line:

26
00:00:57,489 --> 00:00:59,924
"a mercenary, not ideologically driven."

27
00:01:01,227 --> 00:01:02,620
Hold on to that word.

28
00:01:02,996 --> 00:01:05,014
It explains almost everything that

29
00:01:05,027 --> 00:01:05,678
follows.

